<h2>From the BIFR to the NCLT</h2>
<p>Ganesh Benzoplast had passed through the sick-company regime of the Sick Industrial Companies (Special Provisions) Act, 1985, emerging with a sanctioned rehabilitation scheme. Clause 11.11 of that scheme dealt with STC's claim — listing it as a contingent liability, not as the crystallised debt of an unsecured creditor. STC never challenged that classification.</p>

<p>In 2018, STC nonetheless filed CP (IB) 1975/MB/2018 under Section 9 of the IBC. The NCLT, on 13 February 2020, disposed of the petition by directing payment of Rs 2,18,88,421 — 25 per cent of the principal dues, mirroring the scheme's treatment. Six years later, on 21 January 2026, the NCLAT reversed and restored the petition to the NCLT's file. The company appealed.</p>

<h2>The Supreme Court's reasoning</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>The demand sat within the scheme</strong> — STC's demand was issued within the scheme's seven-year window, and its claim was never crystallised outside the scheme's terms — a feature the NCLAT ignored (para 11).</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>No opting out</strong> — Relying on Modi Rubber Ltd v. Continental Carbon India Ltd, the Court held a creditor cannot elect to wait out the scheme and then claim free of it (para 12).</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Scheme finality</strong> — Fertilizer Corporation of India Ltd v. Coromandel Sacks Pvt Ltd applied: the sanctioned scheme's treatment of a claim binds the claimant (para 13).</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Nothing left to adjudicate</strong> — STC was not entitled to 25 per cent in instalments from 2015 as it claimed; the company's offer had been acted upon, the maximum amount stood paid, and no surviving debt remained to carry a Section 9 petition (paras 16-17).</div>
</div>

<p>The appeal was allowed, the NCLAT judgment set aside, and the NCLT's order of 13 February 2020 restored, with parties bearing their own costs (para 18).</p>

<h2>Why the decision matters</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Creditor's position</th><th>Consequence after this ruling</th></tr>
<tr><td>Claim classified and treated under a sanctioned scheme, unchallenged</td><td>Bound by that treatment; IBC cannot improve it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Scheme amount received in full</td><td>No surviving operational debt; Section 9 petition fails</td></tr>
<tr><td>Classification disputed at the time of sanction</td><td>Remedy was to challenge the scheme then, not insolvency later</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="check">
<p><strong>For operational creditors:</strong> the time to fight a restructuring scheme's treatment of a claim is when the scheme is framed and sanctioned. Silence at that stage is acquiescence, and the IBC's summary jurisdiction will not reopen what a binding collective arrangement has closed. For corporate debtors emerging from legacy schemes, this judgment is a shield worth pleading at the Section 9 threshold itself.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>The decision continues a consistent Supreme Court theme: insolvency proceedings are not a debt-collection device, and collective arrangements — BIFR schemes yesterday, resolution plans today — derive their value from finality. A creditor who participates in, or stands by during, such an arrangement takes its benefits and its haircuts together.</p>
</div>
